<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Andean civilizations</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Andean civilizations were complex societies of many indigenous peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, stretching 4,000 km along the Andes from southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> to north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> and northwest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caral">Caral</a> or Norte Chico civilisation of coastal Peru, dating back to 3500 BCE, is the oldest known civilisation in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>; later came Chavín, Paracas, Nazca, Moche, Wari, Tiwanaku and Chimor. They had no writing, wheeled vehicles or money, recorded information on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quipu">quipu</a> and domesticated crops such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potatoes</a>, quinoa and coca. From 1438 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Incas</a> united most of them in one empire, before the Spanish conquest of the 16th century.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Archaeologists believe that Andean civilizations first developed on the narrow Pacific coastal plain. The earliest evidence for settlement in the Andean region dates to about 15,000 BCE, in what archaeologists call the Lithic Period. In the following preceramic period plants began to be widely cultivated, and the Caral-Supe civilization emerged, lasting until 1800 BCE; religious centres such as the Kotosh Religious Tradition appeared in the highlands. Scholars count the Andean civilization among those deemed pristine, meaning that it developed independently of other civilizations.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Ceramic Period brought several complex societies. The Chavín culture, a religious movement that apparently began in the highlands, centred on the ritual site of Chavín de Huántar. The Paracas culture was known for extremely complex textiles, and its successor, the Nazca culture, produced ceramics, textiles, the geoglyphs known as the Nazca lines and underground <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aqueduct">aqueducts</a> called puquios that still function. Scholars contend that the Moche of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> were probably a group of autonomous polities with a common elite culture rather than a single state. Later the Wari and Tiwanaku held large areas of the highlands and coast, and the Chimú of Chimor, with its capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chan-chan">Chan Chan</a>, arose about 900 CE.</p><p>In 1438 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Incas</a> founded Tahuantinsuyu, the largest empire the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> had seen, ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cuzco</a>; it grew from the Kingdom of Cuzco, founded around 1230. The Chimú were conquered around 1470 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupac-inca-yupanqui">Topa Inca Yupanqui</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aymara">Aymara</a> kingdoms, established around 1151 after the collapse of Tiwanaku, were absorbed in 1477. The Timoto Cuica of Venezuela remained outside the Inca orbit. In the 16th century Spanish colonisers subjugated the indigenous kingdoms, and the last Inca stronghold was conquered in 1572.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The environment was severe. On the desert coast agriculture depended on irrigation of river valleys; in the mountains thin soils, cold and steep terrain called for terraces, microclimates and selective breeding. Farmers grew several crops at different elevations, and states established colonies at different heights in what is called the vertical archipelago. The Andeans cultivated an estimated 70 plants, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potatoes</a>, quinoa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomato">tomatoes</a>, chile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">peppers</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, coca and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peanut">peanuts</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. They domesticated llamas and guinea pigs.</p><p>Most of the region's economies were organised on reciprocity and redistribution rather than money and markets, although <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> axe-monies and Spondylus shells served as exchange in some areas, especially coastal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a>. Scholars differ on whether the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quipu">quipu</a>, a system of knotted and coloured strings, recorded only numbers or could also convey narrative; few survive and they have not been fully deciphered.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Andean civilizations” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
